    "done_when": "reckon/cli.py _recorded_fleet_times returns, for each run, its current state and the observed_at of the first event in the trailing run of events whose to_state equals that state, so a re-emitted baseline for an unchanged state never moves the time, while a genuine change of state does. The docstring states exactly that. A test in tests/test_follow_rearm_continues.py writes a transition into working followed by three baseline re-emissions of working, and asserts the replayed row carries the transition time. A second case, working then blocked then working, asserts the row carries the second entry into working. The declared mutation, keeping the last event naming the run, fails the first case with its log recorded. The follow, ticker, watch and row-policy files named in the after_suite command of r-20260929T093259536095-follow-boundary-snaps-to-a-line show zero added failures by id against the dispatch base, with baseline_suite and after_suite as one-line JSON carrying revision, tree, full command, exit_status, absolute log_path, completed, failure_count and failure_ids, each log headed with its revision and tree. Only _recorded_fleet_times and its docstring change in reckon/cli.py",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "each row the follower replays carries the time its run entered its current state, the first event of the unbroken trailing sequence of that state, never a later re-emitted baseline",
